WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.211
Failure paths are where logging is most useful and most risky.

2
00:00:04.211 --> 00:00:11.484
Exceptions, error codes, failed parsing, rejected authentication, unexpected states, and cleanup failures all create pressure to record more detail.

3
00:00:11.484 --> 00:00:13.781
Safe logging keeps that detail controlled.

4
00:00:13.781 --> 00:00:16.460
Separate user-facing errors from internal diagnostics.

5
00:00:16.460 --> 00:00:21.820
A user-facing message may say that an operation failed or input was invalid.

6
00:00:21.820 --> 00:00:28.327
Internal logs may include component names, safe identifiers, operation context, and error categories that help maintainers investigate.

7
00:00:28.327 --> 00:00:30.241
Even internal logs need limits.

8
00:00:30.241 --> 00:00:37.897
Do not include raw secrets, full request bodies, sensitive records, or broad object dumps just because an exception was caught.

9
00:00:37.897 --> 00:00:43.256
Exception messages from libraries should be reviewed before they are logged in production contexts.

10
00:00:43.256 --> 00:00:45.553
Logging should not destabilize cleanup paths.

11
00:00:45.553 --> 00:00:50.912
Destructors and stack unwinding paths should avoid operations that can throw or block unpredictably.

12
00:00:50.912 --> 00:00:58.185
If cleanup itself can fail, the project should define how that condition is reported without hiding the original failure.

13
00:00:58.185 --> 00:00:59.334
Preserve diagnostic usefulness.

14
00:00:59.334 --> 00:01:05.841
Record what operation failed, where it happened, a safe correlation value, and enough classification to support triage.

15
00:01:05.841 --> 00:01:11.201
Avoid replacing a meaningful original error with a vague logging failure or secondary exception.

16
00:01:11.201 --> 00:01:14.646
The boundary question is: who should see this detail?

17
00:01:14.646 --> 00:01:16.943
Maintainers may need structured diagnostic context.

18
00:01:16.943 --> 00:01:20.388
Users and external callers usually need a safe summary.

19
00:01:20.388 --> 00:01:23.833
Good logging design serves both audiences without mixing them.

20
00:01:23.833 --> 00:01:28.809
Exception-heavy code should also avoid logging the same failure at every layer.

21
00:01:28.809 --> 00:01:32.254
Repeated logging can create noise and duplicate sensitive context.

22
00:01:32.254 --> 00:01:39.145
Decide which boundary records the diagnostic event and which layers add structured context or simply propagate the error.

23
00:01:39.145 --> 00:01:43.738
When cleanup fails, record the cleanup issue without losing the primary error.

24
00:01:43.738 --> 00:01:52.160
A secondary diagnostic should help maintainers understand the full sequence while preserving the original cause and keeping secrets out of the event.
